WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.251
Which direction is a group, a column
going down or a row going across?

00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:06.238
A group goes down.

00:00:06.577 --> 00:00:07.938
A period goes across.

00:00:08.485 --> 00:00:10.420
I picture the group standing in a queue.

00:00:11.193 --> 00:00:15.206
For the main group examples,
similar outer electron arrangements

00:00:15.206 --> 00:00:17.298
explain similar chemical properties.

00:00:17.848 --> 00:00:21.147
Lithium and sodium each
have one outer electron.

00:00:21.527 --> 00:00:25.497
Sodium has three occupied shells,
placing it in period three.

00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:30.013
So lithium and sodium share
their outer electron number, not

00:00:30.031 --> 00:00:31.722
their total number of electrons.

00:00:32.388 --> 00:00:35.586
Exactly, a very useful exam distinction.

00:00:36.021 --> 00:00:40.151
Across a simple period, the proton
number increases and electrons

00:00:40.152 --> 00:00:41.795
are added as the row develops.

00:00:42.118 --> 00:00:46.324
The number of occupied shells stays the
same across those introductory examples.

00:00:47.008 --> 00:00:52.184
My course calls halogens group seven,
but this big table calls them 17.

00:00:52.694 --> 00:00:54.316
Has someone made a printing mistake?

00:00:55.057 --> 00:00:56.509
Two numbering conventions.

00:00:56.882 --> 00:01:01.307
Our school notes use group seven for
halogens and group zero for noble gases.

00:01:01.858 --> 00:01:05.560
Modern full table numbering
calls those groups 17 and 18.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.057
We will label both so
you can recognize them.

00:01:08.852 --> 00:01:12.014
And I should not assume every
modern group number equals

00:01:12.016 --> 00:01:13.692
its number of outer electrons.

00:01:14.432 --> 00:01:14.985
Correct.

00:01:15.184 --> 00:01:17.980
The simple matching rule
needs its main group context.

00:01:18.232 --> 00:01:19.830
Helium also deserves care.

00:01:20.055 --> 00:01:24.574
It has two electrons, a full first
shell, and belongs with the noble gases.

00:01:24.886 --> 00:01:27.622
Most metals occupy the left
and center of the table.

00:01:27.925 --> 00:01:30.214
Most non-metals are
towards the upper right.

00:01:30.616 --> 00:01:34.074
The zigzag boundary helps us
locate the change, but hydrogen

00:01:34.085 --> 00:01:35.709
is a non-metal on the left.

00:01:36.436 --> 00:01:40.824
So position gives a useful clue,
not permission to ignore hydrogen.

00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:42.452
Exactly.

00:01:42.803 --> 00:01:45.775
Here are a copper wire and
a piece of aluminum foil.

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.664
Metals typically conduct
heat and electricity.

00:01:49.096 --> 00:01:52.055
Malleable means they can be
hammered or pressed into shapes.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.834
Ductile means they can
be drawn into wires.

00:01:55.617 --> 00:01:57.606
The foil helps me remember malleable.

00:01:58.022 --> 00:01:59.932
The wire helps with ductile.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.161
Neither word means magnetic.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:03.635
Good catch.

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:08.374
Many metals are shiny when their surfaces
are fresh, and many have high melting

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:10.311
points, but there are exceptions.

00:02:10.822 --> 00:02:15.303
Mercury is liquid at room temperature,
and the alkali metals have unusual

00:02:15.304 --> 00:02:17.005
softness and low densities.

00:02:17.817 --> 00:02:21.055
Non-metals are usually poor
electrical conductors, but

00:02:21.056 --> 00:02:22.764
pencil graphite is an exception.

00:02:23.492 --> 00:02:24.076
Yes.

00:02:24.822 --> 00:02:27.470
Graphite is a form of
carbon that conducts.

00:02:27.803 --> 00:02:30.891
Solid non-metals are often
brittle rather than bendable,

00:02:31.185 --> 00:02:32.945
and many non-metals are gases.

00:02:33.538 --> 00:02:36.917
Avoid saying every member
has every typical property.

00:02:37.639 --> 00:02:39.940
What about silicon near the staircase?

00:02:40.717 --> 00:02:44.487
It is often classed as a metalloid
with a combination of properties.

00:02:45.007 --> 00:02:48.163
Its semiconductor behavior
is useful in electronics.

00:02:48.477 --> 00:02:51.755
Our broad map predicts
patterns, while evidence tells

00:02:51.755 --> 00:02:53.168
us where the exceptions are.

00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:58.509
My improved sentence is, "Metals
typically conduct electricity," rather

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.444
than, "All shiny things are metals."

00:03:00.846 --> 00:03:04.659
Lithium, sodium, and potassium
are alkali metals in group one.

00:03:05.006 --> 00:03:09.628
Their atoms each have one outer electron,
which can be lost during reactions.

00:03:09.986 --> 00:03:13.760
They are soft and reactive, unlike
our sturdy copper wire prop.

00:03:14.429 --> 00:03:16.932
Why are they often shown stored under oil?

00:03:17.751 --> 00:03:20.176
Oil helps protect them
from air and moisture.

00:03:20.585 --> 00:03:24.269
They react with water to produce
hydrogen and a metal hydroxide

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:25.784
solution, which is alkaline.

00:03:26.283 --> 00:03:28.964
These reactions can be
dangerous, so our examples are

00:03:28.964 --> 00:03:31.048
diagrams, not home experiments.

00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:35.871
Does lithium react more vigorously than
potassium because it sits higher up?

00:03:36.625 --> 00:03:37.353
The opposite.

00:03:37.754 --> 00:03:40.488
Reactivity generally
increases down this family.

00:03:40.805 --> 00:03:44.925
Potassium reacts more vigorously than
sodium, which reacts more vigorously

00:03:44.928 --> 00:03:47.094
than lithium under comparable conditions.

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:51.406
More shells means the outer electron
is farther from the nucleus.

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:52.280
Is that the reason?

00:03:53.257 --> 00:03:53.880
Part of it.

00:03:54.413 --> 00:03:58.879
Inner electrons also shield the outer
electron from the nucleus' attraction.

00:03:59.261 --> 00:04:03.539
Greater distance and shielding make
that outer electron easier to lose

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:05.771
despite the increased nuclear charge.

00:04:06.485 --> 00:04:08.486
So I explain easier electron loss.

00:04:09.102 --> 00:04:12.304
Saying bigger atom, more
exciting is not enough.

00:04:13.104 --> 00:04:13.923
Exactly.

00:04:14.227 --> 00:04:19.130
Classroom observations may show lithium
fizzing, sodium moving as a molten ball,

00:04:19.361 --> 00:04:21.723
and potassium igniting with a lilac flame.

00:04:22.191 --> 00:04:25.959
The outcome depends on conditions,
so do not treat an animation

00:04:25.959 --> 00:04:27.736
as a guaranteed safe recipe.

00:04:28.473 --> 00:04:31.402
What about magnesium and
calcium in group two?

00:04:32.193 --> 00:04:35.440
They are alkaline earth metals
with two outer electrons.

00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:38.981
They also react, but their
behaviors differ from group one.

00:04:39.455 --> 00:04:40.000
Magnesium reacts very slowly with cold
water, while calcium reacts more readily.
